@vsm 1.0
// docs/incidents/2026-08-02_clima_nats_observability_incident.vsm
// INCIDENT REPORT — framed per standard IR lifecycle (Identification →
// Containment → Eradication/Remediation → Recovery → Lessons Learned),
// per rmw3's explicit request 2026-08-03.
// Author: Claude [O] + rmw3 (S5), live during response.
// Status: Identification/Containment/Eradication DONE for the triggering
// symptom. Fleet-wide remediation IN PROGRESS (DS, NATS-AUTH-FLEET-AUDIT).
// Recovery NOT STARTED (explicitly gated — see recovery_gate). This
// document doubles as the running lessons-learned draft; final closure
// note gets appended when recovery completes.

incident("CLIMA-NATS-OBSERVABILITY-2026-08-02") {

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // CLASSIFICATION
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  classification: {
    trigger: "rmw3 direct question: 'quiero saber si habran quedado
        recursos vinculados a clima, en el nucleo, que esten comiendo
        recursos?' -- NOT a monitoring alert, NOT an automated detection.
        A human asked a pointed question and that is the only reason this
        surfaced.",
    severity: "No user-facing outage (the nucleus itself stayed up
        throughout). But structural: the algedonic/observability channel
        that is SUPPOSED to catch exactly this class of problem had been
        non-functional in multiple, compounding ways for weeks, and this
        was itself undetected until asked about directly.",
    scope_note: "This report covers the clima-bridge/NATS/observability/
        BBS/nucleus-cleanup/watchdog arc from this session (2026-08-02
        21:00 UTC onward). It does NOT re-litigate earlier same-session
        threads (SOC Wazuh install, thermal governance study, SSD
        migration planning) which are separate, already-documented, and
        not part of this incident's causal chain.",
  }

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // FORENSIC TIMELINE — reconstructed from verified logs, not narrated
  // from memory. Each entry has a source. "??" marks genuinely
  // unresolved timing questions -- not asserted as fact.
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  timeline: [
    "2026-06-12T13:35:01Z -- service_watchdog.py deployed to n02 host cron
        (*/5min). BUG BORN AT DEPLOYMENT: _pct_exec() calls bare 'pct'
        with no PATH guarantee; cron's minimal environment lacks
        /usr/sbin. First FileNotFoundError logged the SAME DAY, minutes
        after deployment. [source: /var/log/vsf/watchdog.log first entry]",

    "2026-07-02T07:50:01Z (approx, last entry) -- vsf-g6-algedonic-sink's
        archive log (/root/algedonic/vsf_algedonic.jsonl) receives its
        last confirmed entries -- including real service_watchdog payloads
        (the pct-error alerts, ironically -- proving delivery DID work at
        this point via SOME path). No entries after this date found.
        ?? WHY it stopped here specifically is not resolved -- predates
        the 07-11 auth migration below, so may be a separate cause. Left
        for DS's fleet audit. [source: vsf_algedonic.jsonl tail]",

    "2026-07-11 -- NATS migrated to token authentication fleet-wide.
        [source: docs/spec/nats_auth_remediation/spec.vsm root_cause.event
        -- pre-existing doc, not discovered this session]. Failure mode
        documented then: 'the systemd unit stays active, nats.py connects
        TCP then fails auth and retries in the background, so NO messages
        flow and nothing crashes. The failure is invisible without connz.'
        This is the SAME shape as everything found in this incident.",

    "2026-07-11 onward -- service_watchdog.py's _publish_algedonic() (no
        token, CONNECT {} with an empty object) begins being rejected by
        NATS with -ERR 'Authorization Violation' on every single call.
        The code never read the post-CONNECT response, so it believed
        every publish succeeded. This is silent from this point forward.
        [inferred: the auth migration date + the confirmed-broken code
        path + the last-successful-delivery date above being pre-migration]",

    "2026-07-14 -- KERNEL-S5-NATS-AUTH (Track B of the same 07-11 root
        cause) closed: kernel S5 channels (covenant/caution/approval/halt)
        fixed to authenticate. Clima bridges (Track A) also fixed same
        window. [source: TASKS.vsm, pre-existing, session 2026-07-14-ds-deploy]
        NOTE: the 'systemic' fix that same spec proposed -- ONE mandatory
        authenticated-connect helper per language, enforced -- was
        explicitly deferred ('land as part of VSL-STANDARDIZATION') and,
        per this incident's evidence, never actually enforced. This is
        the direct ancestor of why service_watchdog.py (written
        separately, raw socket code) was never caught or fixed alongside
        Track A/B.",

    "2026-07-19 -- BBS-DECOMMISSION.spec.vsm written: bbs-api + the
        n02-host poller confirmed dead (messages.db unwritten since
        2026-05-24), vsf-bbs-bridge confirmed to be a live, misleadingly-
        named phi_down actuator (explicitly NOT to be touched). Gated on
        the merkle fix. [pre-existing doc, not discovered this session]",

    "2026-08-01T23:37:29Z -- last confirmed-healthy vsf-s4-clima-bridge
        activity (bundle #993 received) before the outage. [journalctl -u
        vsf-s4-clima-bridge, LXC 101]",

    "2026-08-01T23:45:01Z -- service_watchdog's cron cycle runs.
        check_nats() (the shallow, raw-socket probe -- separate from the
        broken pct-based checks) CORRECTLY detects the NATS problem:
        FAIL(timed out). This is the single correctly-fired, correctly-
        timed detection in this whole incident.",

    "2026-08-01T23:45:06Z -- the code attempts to publish that correct
        alert via _publish_algedonic(). Given the 07-11 auth bug above,
        this publish is REJECTED by NATS, silently (no response check in
        the old code). The one correct signal never leaves the host.",

    "2026-08-01T23:47:13Z -- LAST successful clima-bridge bundle received
        before tonight's fix. From this instant, ~22h10m of total silence
        begins. [journalctl -u vsf-s4-clima-bridge]",

    "2026-08-01T23:55:06Z -- check_nats() fires FAIL a second time,
        correctly. Same silent publish failure.",

    "2026-08-01T23:55Z -> 2026-08-02T20:00Z (~20h) -- check_nats()'s
        shallow probe (TCP connect + read INFO banner only) reports OK
        continuously. This is a FALSE ALL-CLEAR: NATS's accept queue was
        degraded enough to still hand out INFO banners to bare probes
        while genuinely unable to serve authenticated application clients
        (vsf-s4-clima-bridge kept failing to connect the entire window).
        Root cause of the underlying NATS/106 degradation itself (systemd
        D-Bus reporting 'Transport endpoint is not connected', accept
        queue Recv-Q 4097 > backlog 4096) was never separately isolated --
        see open_questions.",

    "2026-08-02T17:55:00Z -- bbs-api (LXC 106, already-deprecated 2026-05-24)
        begins throwing explicit 'OSError: Too many open files' -- a slow
        file-descriptor leak (orphaned poller hitting dead endpoints every
        ~30s, never cleanly closing connections) finally crosses the
        container's fd ceiling. 668 occurrences logged by 21:35:11.
        Plausible contributing/compounding cause of 106's degraded state,
        alongside whatever originally broke NATS 07-11-onward.",

    "2026-08-02T20:00:06Z onward -- check_nats() resumes correctly firing
        FAIL every cycle (the false-all-clear window ends) -- still never
        delivered (same auth bug, still unfixed at this point).",

    "2026-08-02T21:29Z -- THIS SESSION'S live response begins, triggered
        by rmw3's clima-resource question (see classification.trigger).",

    "2026-08-02T21:56:47Z -- pct reboot 106 (targeted, not a full n02
        host reboot -- rmw3's explicit scope call, minimizing blast
        radius given the host was already under load from unrelated work
        this session). NATS clean immediately after: accept queue back to
        0, vsf-s4-clima-bridge connects on attempt 1, subscribes cleanly.
        CONTAINMENT achieved for the triggering symptom.",

    "2026-08-02T22:00Z-23:30Z (approx) -- BBS fully decommissioned per the
        pre-existing 2026-07-19 spec (archived, stopped, disabled, unit
        files removed, state.db reconciled). vsf-bbs-bridge deliberately
        preserved per that spec's own finding -- confirmed live and
        healthy throughout.",

    "2026-08-03T00:30Z-01:15Z (approx) -- rmw3 asked for the rename
        recommended in the 07-19 spec: vsf-bbs-bridge ->
        vsf-s3-phidown-actuator (operational-level scope, consumer sweep
        caught 4 more references incl. a LIVE deployed
        vsf-recovery-exercise gate). Then: nucleus dead-code removal
        (bbs.rs, confirmed zero references) + misleading log-string fix
        ('published to BBS' -> 'logged (embedded)'), built on LXC 108,
        deployed atomically to 101, verified live.",

    "2026-08-03T00:xxZ -- rmw3's staged container plan executed: only
        101/104/106/107/108/109/110 running; 105/111/112/113/114 (S1s +
        jupyter) and 115/116/117 (SOC) deliberately stopped -- 'antes de
        meter ningun s1, cableamos y mapeamos todo el hardware.'",

    "2026-08-03T01:30Z-02:10Z (approx) -- docs/spec/sensor_omega_
        integration/spec_2026-08-02.vsm written: root-cause of WHY the
        one correct 23:45:06 alert went unseen (bug_A: 7 weeks of
        pct-PATH false noise; bug_B: the 20h false-all-clear probe-depth
        gap), plus the general architectural framing (existence-checking
        vs behavior-checking, the R^128/Omega classification taxonomy).",

    "2026-08-03T02:2xZ -- STAGE2A execution: service_watchdog.py's
        pct-PATH bug fixed + verified with a REAL run (not a code read).
        That run immediately surfaced TWO more previously-invisible bugs:
        ollama monitored on the wrong node (101 instead of its real home,
        110, since 2026-07-13) and curl missing on 110. Both fixed.",

    "2026-08-03T02:3x-02:5xZ -- Deepening check_nats() (STAGE2A-NATS-
        PROBE-DEPTH) led directly to discovering and fixing the auth bug
        in _publish_algedonic() itself (see the 07-11 timeline entries
        above) -- confirmed via a real NATS protocol test
        (unauthenticated CONNECT -> -ERR 'Authorization Violation',
        confirmed live) and via a ledger query (zero rows ever from
        source='service_watchdog' across the entire ~7-week history).
        Fixed: token added, response-checking added, verified with a real
        publish + protocol-level confirmation.",

    "2026-08-03T02:55Z -- rmw3's strategic call: stop digging linearly,
        delegate a systematic fleet-wide audit to DS (NATS-AUTH-FLEET-
        AUDIT dispatched), Claude+rmw3 supervise. DS actively working as
        of this document's writing.",
  ],

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // IR LIFECYCLE PHASES
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  phase_identification: {
    status: "DONE for the triggering symptom, ONGOING for the fleet-wide
        blast radius (DS's audit).",
    how_it_actually_happened: "NOT via any automated detector -- every
        automated detector in the path either never fired correctly
        (service_watchdog: silent auth-rejection since 07-11) or fired
        into a channel already saturated with unrelated noise
        (14,700+/7wk false pct-error alarms). Identification happened
        because rmw3 asked a specific, well-targeted question about a
        specific subsystem (clima). This is itself the primary lesson --
        see lessons_learned.",
  },

  phase_containment: {
    status: "DONE.",
    actions: [
      "Targeted pct reboot of LXC 106 only (not a full n02 host reboot) --
       deliberate scope-minimization given the host was already under
       load this session from unrelated work.",
      "Container triage to an essential-only set (101/104/106/107/108/
       109/110) -- reduces the surface being reasoned about while the
       nucleus gets fully mapped, per rmw3's own staged plan.",
    ],
  },

  phase_eradication_remediation: {
    status: "IN PROGRESS. Local/session-scope items done and verified;
        fleet-wide scope delegated to DS, not yet reported back.",
    done_this_session: [
      "vsf-s4-clima-bridge crash-loop -- root-caused (NATS/106 degraded)
       and resolved via the 106 reboot; NRestarts stopped climbing,
       clean reconnect verified.",
      "bbs-api + vsf-bbs-poller -- fully decommissioned per the
       pre-existing 2026-07-19 spec, archived before deletion, verified
       nothing else broke.",
      "vsf-bbs-bridge -> vsf-s3-phidown-actuator -- renamed at the
       operational level, 4 additional live/repo consumers found+fixed in
       a sweep the first pass missed.",
      "nucleus bbs.rs dead code removed + misleading log strings fixed --
       built on LXC 108, deployed atomically to 101, verified live.",
      "service_watchdog.py -- FOUR distinct bugs found and fixed in one
       pass: pct-PATH resolution (7wk of false alarms), ollama monitored
       on the wrong node, curl missing on the ollama host, and the
       algedonic-publish auth bug (the deepest one -- zero real delivery
       in this watchdog's entire history until fixed tonight).",
    ],
    delegated_in_progress: [
      "NATS-AUTH-FLEET-AUDIT (DS) -- systematic sweep for the same
       auth-bypass bug class across every NATS-connecting component in
       the repo, not just the ones found by accident tonight.",
    ],
  },

  phase_recovery: {
    status: "NOT STARTED -- explicitly gated.",
    recovery_gate: "rmw3's own staged plan: essential containers only
        UNTIL the nucleus is completely mapped and wired (this incident's
        remediation IS that mapping exercise). Only then: reintegrate S1s
        (105 bbh-prod, 111 bbh-lab, 112 clima, 113 econ, 114 jupyter) and
        bring the SOC stack (115/116/117) back up. NOT to be started
        before DS's fleet audit reports back and any production-touching
        fixes it proposes get reviewed.",
  },

  phase_lessons_learned: {
    status: "DRAFT -- this section is live during the incident per
        rmw3's request, will get a closing pass once recovery completes.",

    finding_1_detection_is_not_delivery: "A correctly-firing detector
        (check_nats()) and a correctly-delivered alert are TWO SEPARATE
        THINGS that this incident conflated. The detection logic was
        correct at the moment that mattered (23:45:06); the delivery path
        had been silently broken for weeks before that and stayed broken
        for weeks after. Any future observability work (STAGE2B/2C/2D,
        the generic liveness scanner) must verify end-to-end DELIVERY,
        not just that a check function returns the right boolean.",

    finding_2_noise_defeats_signal_even_when_correct: "The single correct
        alert in this whole incident (23:45:06, 2026-08-01) was
        technically fired at the right time with the right content. It
        still went unseen -- first because it never got past a broken
        publish path, and even in a counterfactual where it had, it would
        have landed in a channel already saturated with 14,700+ identical
        false 'everything is down' alarms from the SAME watchdog's other
        checks. A monitoring system with a high false-positive rate is
        operationally equivalent to no monitoring at all for anything
        that actually matters.",

    finding_3_local_resilience_hides_failure: "systemd's Restart=always +
        WatchdogSec on vsf-s4-clima-bridge kept the SERVICE existing
        (satisfying any existence-check) for the entire 22h it was
        useless. The mechanism designed to make the system resilient is
        the same mechanism that made the failure invisible to anything
        checking only 'does it exist / is it active'. See
        docs/spec/sensor_omega_integration/spec_2026-08-02.vsm section 2
        for the fuller architectural treatment.",

    finding_4_a_known_bug_class_recurred: "This entire incident's deepest
        root cause (NATS auth added 07-11, silent rejection for any
        client not passing the token correctly) had ALREADY happened
        once, to different components, and was ALREADY diagnosed and
        partially fixed by 07-14. The proposed systemic fix (mandate one
        authenticated-connect helper, enforce it) was deferred and never
        landed. service_watchdog.py -- written independently, using its
        own raw-socket NATS code -- walked directly into the same hole
        seven weeks later. A point-fix without an enforcement mechanism
        is a fix for ONE instance of a bug CLASS, not the class itself.",

    finding_5_asking_beats_alerting_right_now: "Every piece of this
        incident that got found tonight was found because rmw3 asked a
        specific, pointed question -- not because any automated system
        raised it. That is not a criticism of rmw3's engagement (the
        opposite -- it worked), it is the sharpest possible evidence that
        the automated layer has a real gap that needs the STAGE2
        remediation plan (already scoped in
        docs/spec/sensor_omega_integration/spec_2026-08-02.vsm) to close.",

    open_questions_status_2026_08_03: [
      "RESOLVED (DS, NATS-AUTH-FLEET-AUDIT): vsf-g6-algedonic-sink stopped
       2026-07-02 not because of auth -- it was tailing a jsonl written by
       vsf_algedonic_subscriber.py, which has NO systemd unit and is
       INACTIVE. Not the same bug class as the rest of this incident, just
       a redundant/stale instrument pointed at a dead source. The real
       channel (vsf_s5_agent.py) was never broken.",
      "RESOLVED (DS): end-to-end delivery confirmed for s3_star and
       s5_agent -- both authenticate correctly and their events land in
       the real ledger (verified via fresh operator_inbox.log entries and
       ledger rows with source='s3_star').",
      "PARTIALLY ANSWERED (Claude, 2026-08-03, netdata historical query):
       WHY did LXC 106 degrade in the first place. No discrete
       error-level log event found at the exact onset (2026-08-01
       23:37-23:45) -- checked journalctl -p warning and grepped for
       dbus/oom/resource-exhaustion strings across the whole prior boot,
       nothing at that timestamp. But netdata's retained history shows
       disk_util.sda averaged 83.6% and system.io_some_pressure averaged
       ~65-75% (10s/60s/300s) during that exact 20-minute window, versus
       ~46% disk utilization at an earlier same-evening baseline
       (20:00-20:20 UTC, ~3.5h before onset). Correlated, not proven
       causal -- but consistent with, not a new/separate finding from,
       the 'n02 host I/O is the dominant/shared root cause' pattern
       already established across this whole session's other incidents
       (thermal_resource_governance, the SOC install saga, the clima I/O
       footprint finding). Treated as the working explanation rather than
       a fully closed question -- the real test is whether this class of
       silent degradation stops recurring once the already-planned SSD
       migration removes the host's single-spinning-disk bottleneck
       entirely. If it recurs post-migration, that would rule this out
       and point to something else.",
    ],
  },
}
